{
  "id": "grammar.b1.arpadhaz.03.vonatkozoi-nevmas-esetei",
  "title": "Case Forms of aki: akit, akinek, akik",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "*aki* behaves like any other Hungarian noun-like word: it takes case endings depending on the job it does inside its OWN clause — not the job its antecedent does in the main sentence. So far you've only seen *aki* as a subject (*aki uralkodott* — who ruled). Now meet its other everyday forms: *akit* (whom, as a direct object) and *akinek* (to whom, or whose, marking a dative or possessor role), plus the plural *akik* and *akiket*."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "aki's case forms",
      "rows": [
        ["aki", "who (subject, singular)"],
        ["akit", "whom (object, singular)"],
        ["akinek", "to whom / whose (dative or possessor, singular)"],
        ["akik", "who (subject, plural)"],
        ["akiket", "whom (object, plural)"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "title": "Examples",
      "items": [
        { "spanish": "IV. Béla, akit a tatárjárás sújtott a legjobban, az ország újjáépítésébe kezdett.", "english": "Béla IV, whom the Mongol invasion hit hardest, began rebuilding the country." },
        { "spanish": "Az ispán, akinek a megye igazgatása volt a feladata, a király nevében ítélkezett.", "english": "The ispán, whose task was administering the county, judged in the king's name." },
        { "spanish": "Azok a nemesek, akik a várak építését szorgalmazták, jól látták a jövő veszélyeit.", "english": "Those nobles who urged the building of castles saw the dangers ahead clearly." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "Work out the case of *aki* from inside its own clause, ignoring the main sentence. In *az ispán, akinek a megye igazgatása volt a feladata*, *az ispán* is the subject of the whole sentence, but inside the relative clause *akinek* is a possessor (*akinek a feladata* = \"whose task\"), so it takes the *-nek* ending — the two clauses are grammatically independent of each other."
    }
  ]
}

